# State:  28 lines, 23 key lines, 5 blank, 11 unique keys, mode 600 root:root,
#         mtime 2026-08-13 22:12
#
# On the other four nodes this path is a symlink to /etc/pve/priv/authorized_keys.
# On grey-server it was a standalone regular file, so inbound root SSH to this
# node read the content below and never read the cluster file. That is why the
# 2026-08-15 cluster key cleanup did not take effect here, and why
# SHA256:Utepy… (line 6) still authorised root on this node afterwards.
#
# REDACTED. Every key blob is replaced by a redaction token. Approved identity
# labels are published as they are in the SSH Authorized Key Cleanup -
# 2026-07-14 record. This is a structural record of what the file held and in
# what order. It is not restorable material, by design.
#
# A token is stable within this file: the same token always means the same key,
# which is what makes the duplication below legible.
#
# Line 2 is grey-server's own node key, carrying the hostname the machine had
# before it was renamed. The sibling file
# grey-server-pve-authorized_keys-2026-08-15 renders that same key as
# <REMOVED_STALE_ROOT_KEY>, on the mistaken reading that it belonged to no
# current node. Ticket 10 corrected that and reinstated the key in the cluster
# file under the comment root@grey-server.
#
# The content is the pre-cleanup cluster file minus the
